        <li>The 30-30-30 rule: 30g protein within 30 minutes of waking, then 30 minutes of low-intensity movement</li>
        <li>The protein component is well-supported by science — especially for men over 40 focused on muscle preservation</li>
        <li>The strict 30-minute timing window is overstated — protein timing is forgiving, and a protein breakfast within an hour or two of waking works just as well</li>
        <li>The movement component is genuinely beneficial — a brisk morning walk improves blood sugar regulation, supports BDNF production, and builds a sustainable daily habit</li>
        <li>For men over 40, this routine is more useful than fasted training — because the goal has shifted from fat burning to muscle preservation</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>I'm 65. I hadn't heard of the 30-30-30 rule until recently — I don't spend much time on social media, and honestly that's where most of these trends live and die. But when I looked at the actual components, something interesting happened: the science held up better than I expected.</p>

    <p>Not perfectly. The 30-minute window claim is marketing, not physiology. But the underlying logic — protein in the morning, followed by movement — is exactly what the research on muscle preservation for men over 40 points toward. The trend got lucky and stumbled onto something real.</p>

    <p>Here's the honest breakdown of what works, what's overstated, and why the fasted workout advice you may have followed for years is worth reconsidering at this stage of life.</p>

